{
  "dom_id": "step-q31968",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nW=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Z=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $W$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =W\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $Y$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $Z$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $W$ and $Z$ are elementary matrices.",
